hi this is presh talwalkar  warren buffett once challenged bill  gates to a special dice game like the  following  the dice each had six faces that were  equally likely to show up but the  numbers were different than a standard  dice  one of the dice had all threes on all of  its six faces  another one had zero on two of its faces  and four on four of its faces  a third dice had an equal number of ones  and fives  and a fourth dice had two on four's  faces and six on two of its faces  so here's how the game works you and i  are each going to pick a different one  of these dice we're not going to pick  the same ones we're going to pick  different dice  we're then going to roll against each  other and the person who rolls a higher  number gets a point  we then repeat that many many times so  that the person who rolls the highest  number of points over many roles is  going to win the game  i'm going to graciously offer you to let  you pick your die first which one do you  want  give this problem a try and when you're  ready keep watching the video for the  solution  so let's analyze this game suppose you  pick the dice that has all threes  what i'm going to do is i'm going to  pick the dice which has zero on two of  its faces and four on four of its faces  what's going to happen when we roll  against each other  well if i roll a zero which happens two  out of the six times i'm gonna lose to  you because you're always gonna roll a  three  however  in the four out of the six times that i  roll a four i'm actually going to beat  you  so i'm going to beat you two-thirds of  the time so if you pick a dice which has  all threes you're gonna lose to me  two-thirds of the time  so now suppose you know this strategy  and you say well why don't i pick the  dice that has two zeros and four fours  well if you're going to pick that dice  then i'm going to go ahead and pick the  dice which has  three ones and three fives  it's a little harder to analyze what  happens when we roll these two dice  against each other but it's not too  complicated  now i'm going to win if i roll a five  because any time i roll a five it's  higher than you rolling a zero or a four  so in three out of the six cases i'm  going to roll a five and i'm guaranteed  to win  now when i roll the one in three out of  the six cases  i'm only going to beat you in the two  out of six cases that you roll a zero as  well  so in summary  i'm going to win 3 6 times 1 when i roll  a 5 plus 3 6 when i roll a 0 when i roll  the 1 times 2 6 when you roll a zero  so this nets out to two thirds of the  time i'm going to win so once again your  die that you pick is going to lose to my  die two-thirds of the time  this argument continues let's suppose  you picked the die that i just had well  in that case i'm going to pick the next  die which has  two on four of his faces and six on two  of his faces we can do a similar  calculation i'm going to win 2 out of  the 6 times when i roll a 6 guaranteed  and i'm also going to win if i roll a 2  which is 4 6 of the time against your 1  which happens 3 6 of the time  so once again i'm going to beat your die  two-thirds of the time  finally you say all right i'll pick the  die you just had and then i'm going to  go ahead and say well i'm going to pick  the die that has all threes now i'm  going to win whenever i roll  whenever you roll a 2 which happens 4 6  of the time so once again your die is  losing to my die two-thirds of the time  so what we've shown  is that each die is actually going to  lose two-thirds of the time to another  die  and this is what's known as  non-transitive dice  each die can be beaten like another like  a game of rock paper scissors this is a  cyclical game and that's why it's  non-transitive  the first person who selects the die is  always going to be at a disadvantage to  someone who picks this second because  each die can be beaten by another  as the story goes warren buffett  challenged bill gates to this and said  go ahead why don't you select first  bill gates was smart enough to say let  me inspect the dice and he figured out  that there was something weird going on  with the dice  did you figure it out  thanks for watching this video please  subscribe to my channel i make videos on  math and game theory you can catch me on  my blog mind your decisions which you  can follow on facebook google plus and  patreon you can catch me on social media  at preshtalwalker and if you like this  video please check out my books there  are links in the video description

